                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The Legend of Bean is a fantasy role-playing game developed by Windward_Birds and published by XNZONE. Built in the style of classic RPGMaker adventures, the game delivers a nostalgic journey that draws inspiration from traditional Japanese RPGs. Rather than focusing on flashy innovations or complex mechanics, it aims to provide an accessible story-driven experience filled with exploration, turn-based combat, and a cast of characters whose adventures gradually unfold across a world troubled by political tensions and looming conflict.\n\nThe story follows Kaim, a wandering mercenary whose travels unexpectedly place him at the center of a larger struggle. After meeting a mysterious young woman who is later revealed to be a princess, Kaim becomes involved in a mission that extends far beyond a simple escort assignment. The narrative explores themes of loyalty, friendship, and responsibility as the characters travel through dangerous territories and confront the consequences of a war between rival nations. While the plot relies on familiar fantasy elements, it maintains enough charm and momentum to keep players invested in the outcome of the journey.\n\nCombat is based on a traditional turn-based system that prioritizes strategy over reflexes. Players control a compact party of heroes, managing their abilities, equipment, and resources while facing a variety of enemies. The game features dozens of monster types, each contributing to a steady sense of progression as new challenges emerge throughout the adventure. Battles are straightforward to understand, making the game approachable for newcomers while still providing enough tactical depth to satisfy fans of classic RPG mechanics.\n\nExploration plays a significant role in the overall experience. Players travel through towns, wilderness areas, and dungeons designed with the familiar visual style associated with RPGMaker projects. The environments are detailed enough to establish a strong sense of place, and the world feels cohesive despite the game's modest production scale. Hidden treasures, NPC interactions, and story events encourage players to take their time and immerse themselves in the setting rather than simply rushing from one objective to the next.\n\nOne of the game's most appealing qualities is its emphasis on character interaction. The dialogue frequently mixes lighthearted humor with more serious story moments, helping the party members develop distinct personalities. These exchanges add warmth to the adventure and create a stronger connection between the player and the cast. Although some localization imperfections occasionally affect the flow of conversations, the overall writing succeeds in giving the characters enough individuality to remain memorable.\n\nThe audiovisual presentation is simple but effective. The soundtrack complements the fantasy atmosphere with a variety of musical themes that support exploration, combat, and emotional story scenes. Visually, the game embraces a retro-inspired aesthetic that will feel immediately familiar to long-time RPG enthusiasts. While it lacks the graphical sophistication of larger productions, the presentation demonstrates a clear passion for the genre and supports the game's adventurous tone.\n\nThe Legend of Bean does not attempt to redefine role-playing games, but it succeeds in delivering a heartfelt adventure that understands the strengths of classic RPG design. Its enjoyable combat system, engaging fantasy setting, memorable characters, and steady pacing create an experience that will appeal primarily to players who appreciate traditional RPGMaker titles. Those seeking groundbreaking mechanics may find it somewhat conventional, but fans of old-school role-playing games will discover a charming and worthwhile adventure that offers several enjoyable hours of gameplay.\n\nRating: 7/10",
